---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import CreditCardPayoffCalculator from '@components/islands/CreditCardPayoffCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'credit-card-payoff-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How long will it take to pay off my credit cards?', answer: 'Enter what you can put toward your cards each month, then add each card\'s balance, minimum payment and interest rate. The calculator builds a debt-avalanche plan and shows when every card is clear, what the whole plan costs, and the payment schedule for each card.' },
  { question: 'What is the debt avalanche method?', answer: 'You pay the minimum on every card and put every spare dollar toward the card with the highest interest rate. When that card clears, its whole payment rolls onto the next-highest rate. It clears the debt for the least total interest of any order, because the most expensive balance is always the one shrinking fastest.' },
  { question: 'Is the avalanche better than the snowball method?', answer: 'Mathematically, yes — the avalanche always costs less interest. The snowball method targets the smallest balance first instead, which clears individual cards sooner and can be easier to stick with. If the motivation of closing a card is what keeps you going, the snowball\'s slightly higher cost may be worth paying.' },
  { question: 'What happens if I only pay the minimum?', answer: 'Minimum payments are designed to keep you in debt as long as possible. On a high balance, paying only the minimum can take over a decade and cost more in interest than the original purchase. Every dollar above the minimum attacks the balance directly.' },
  { question: 'Why does my payment change part-way through the plan?', answer: 'Because a freed-up payment is immediately redirected. When a card needs only part of its usual payment in its final month, the remainder goes to the next card that same month — and once the card is gone, its entire payment does. That is why the schedule shows each card\'s payment stepping up over time.' },
  { question: 'Should I use a balance transfer?', answer: 'A 0% introductory-APR balance transfer can save a lot of interest if you clear the balance before the promotional period ends. Weigh the transfer fee (typically 3–5%) and be sure the payment you can afford will actually pay it off in time — the rate jumps once the intro period is over.' },
  { question: 'Can you pay off a credit card with another credit card?', answer: 'Not directly — no issuer accepts another credit card as payment for a bill. The two indirect routes are a balance transfer, where the new issuer clears the old balance for a fee of roughly 3-5%, usually at 0% for an introductory period; and a cash advance, which is almost always a mistake because it carries a higher APR, adds a fee, and charges interest from day one with no grace period.' },
  { question: 'When should I pay my credit card — the statement date or the due date?', answer: 'Both dates matter for different reasons. Paying the full statement balance by the due date is what avoids interest. Paying before the statement closing date is what lowers the balance reported to the credit bureaus, because issuers report the balance as it stands when the statement closes. Someone who pays in full every month can still show high utilisation if they always pay after closing.' },
  { question: 'Does paying off a credit card hurt your credit score?', answer: 'Paying the balance down helps, because utilisation is one of the largest factors in a score and it responds within a billing cycle or two. What can work against you is closing the card afterwards: that removes its limit from your total available credit, raising utilisation on the cards you keep, and over time it shortens the average age of your accounts. A cleared card left open usually helps more than a closed one.' },
  { question: 'Is it bad to pay off a credit card early, or should I carry a balance?', answer: 'Paying early is never penalised, and carrying a balance does not build credit. The card reports activity whether or not a balance is left on it, so paying the statement in full each month gives the same reporting without the interest. The idea that a balance helps your score is a myth that costs real money.' },
  { question: 'Which credit card should I pay off first?', answer: 'The one with the highest interest rate, if you want to pay the least in total — that is the debt avalanche, and it is what this calculator plans. If closing a card quickly is what keeps you motivated, the snowball method targets the smallest balance first; it costs a little more interest but is easier for some people to stick with.' },
  { question: 'Can you pay off a loan with a credit card?', answer: 'Rarely, and it is usually a poor trade. Most lenders do not accept credit cards, third-party payment services charge around 2-3% to route it, and a car or personal loan almost always carries a lower rate than a card. Moving the balance onto a card converts cheaper debt into more expensive debt.' },
  { question: 'How is credit card interest calculated?', answer: 'Card interest is quoted as an APR but usually charged daily on your balance. Divide the APR by 365 for the daily rate, and the issuer applies it to your average balance each day, so carrying a balance even a few days costs interest. Paying the statement in full each month avoids it entirely.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  rail
  intro="Plan the fastest, cheapest way out of credit card debt: enter what you can pay each month and the details of each card, and see when they are all clear and what it costs."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-27' }}
>
  <CreditCardPayoffCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the <strong>monthly budget</strong> you can set aside for credit cards in total.</li>
      <li>Add each card's <strong>balance</strong>, <strong>minimum payment</strong> and <strong>interest rate</strong>. Naming a card is optional, but it makes the plan easier to follow.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate Payoff</strong> to see how long the whole plan takes and what it costs.</li>
      <li>Read the per-card schedule to see exactly what to pay on each card, month by month.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Your budget has to cover every minimum payment before any plan is possible — that is the floor
      the card issuers set. Everything you can add above that floor is what actually clears the debt.
    </p>

    <h2>The debt avalanche method</h2>
    <p>
      With several cards you have one real decision to make: where does the spare money go? The
      avalanche answers it with arithmetic. Pay every card its minimum, then send every remaining
      dollar to the card with the <strong>highest interest rate</strong> — because that is the
      balance charging you the most for each additional month it survives.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the top card clears, its entire payment rolls onto the next-highest rate, which is why the
      plan accelerates: the amount attacking your debt grows every time a card disappears, even
      though your budget never changes. No other payment order clears the same debt for less
      interest.
    </p>

    <h2>How credit card interest works</h2>
    <p>
      Card interest is quoted as an <strong>APR</strong> but charged on your balance, so carrying a
      balance costs money every month. Divide the APR by 12 for the monthly rate: a 22% APR is about
      1.83% a month. Because the charge is based on what you owe, the interest shrinks as the balance
      falls — which is why extra payments compound in your favour. Paying the statement in full each
      month avoids interest entirely.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example: three cards, $500 a month</h2>
    <p>
      Say you owe <strong>$4,600 at 18.99%</strong>, <strong>$3,900 at 19.99%</strong> and
      <strong>$6,000 at 15.99%</strong>, with minimums of $100, $90 and $120. That is
      <strong>$14,500</strong> of debt and <strong>$310</strong> of minimums. Putting
      <strong>$500</strong> a month toward the cards clears all three in
      <strong>38 months</strong> for <strong>$18,971.20</strong> — of which
      <strong>$4,471.20</strong> is interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plan starts by paying $280 on the 19.99% card (its $90 minimum plus the whole $190
      surplus), while the other two get their minimums. That card clears in month 16, and its $280
      moves to the 18.99% card, which now gets $380 a month and clears in month 28. Everything then
      lands on the last card, which takes the full $500 until it is gone in month 38.
    </p>
    <p>
      Watch what the extra money does: the same $500 budget pays off the first card in 16 months, the
      second 12 months later, and the third only 10 months after that. The debt clears faster and
      faster because the payment attacking it keeps growing.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the payment order matters so much</h2>
    <p>
      The minimums alone are a slow, expensive plan. Those same three cards charge
      <strong>$217.71</strong> of interest in the first month, so paying the
      <strong>$310</strong> of minimums leaves only about <strong>$92</strong> attacking a
      <strong>$14,500</strong> debt. It still clears — in <strong>82 months</strong>, or nearly
      seven years, at a cost of <strong>$10,696</strong> in interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      Raising the budget to <strong>$500</strong> — $190 a month more — cuts that to
      <strong>38 months</strong> and <strong>$4,471</strong> of interest. You pay more each month
      and <em>less in total</em>: <strong>$18,971</strong> instead of <strong>$25,196</strong>, a
      saving of over <strong>$6,200</strong>, because every one of those extra dollars goes straight
      at the principal of the most expensive card instead of renting it for another year.
    </p>

    <h2>Strategies to clear it faster</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Avalanche</strong> — highest interest rate first. This is what the calculator plans, and it costs the least total interest.</li>
      <li><strong>Snowball</strong> — smallest balance first for a quick, motivating win, then roll that payment into the next card. It costs a little more but is easier for some people to sustain.</li>
      <li><strong>Balance transfer</strong> — a 0% intro-APR card pauses interest; clear the balance before the promo ends and mind the transfer fee (typically 3–5%).</li>
      <li><strong>Pay biweekly</strong> — half-payments every two weeks make one extra monthly payment a year and cut the balance the interest is charged on.</li>
      <li><strong>Stop adding charges</strong> — a plan only works on a balance that has stopped growing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Can you pay one credit card with another?</h2>
    <p>
      Not directly. No issuer lets you settle one card's bill with another card, because a
      credit card is not a funding source the way a bank account is. There are two ways to
      move a balance from one card to another, and they are not equivalent.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>balance transfer</strong> is the legitimate route. The new issuer pays the old
      card off and the balance moves across, usually at 0% for an introductory period, for a
      fee of about 3–5% of the amount moved. It only saves money if you clear the balance
      before the promotional rate ends, so work backwards from the payment you can actually
      afford and check it finishes in time.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>cash advance</strong> — withdrawing cash on one card to pay another — is almost
      always a mistake. Cash advances usually carry a higher APR than purchases, charge a fee
      of around 3–5%, and skip the grace period entirely, so interest starts the day you take
      the money. You would be swapping a debt for a more expensive one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same reasoning applies to <strong>paying a loan with a credit card</strong>. Most
      lenders do not accept cards, third-party payment services charge 2–3% to do it, and a
      car or personal loan almost always carries a lower rate than a card. Moving that balance
      onto a card converts cheaper debt into more expensive debt.
    </p>

    <h2>When in the month to pay</h2>
    <p>
      Two different dates matter, and they do different jobs. Paying the full statement balance
      by the <strong>due date</strong> is what avoids interest. Paying before the
      <strong>statement closing date</strong> is what lowers the balance your issuer reports to
      the credit bureaus.
    </p>
    <p>
      Issuers report the balance as it stands when the statement closes, not the balance after
      you pay. Someone who clears the card in full every month can still show high utilisation
      if they pay after the statement has already closed. Making a payment a few days before
      the closing date reports a lower figure, which is the number lenders see.
    </p>

    <h2>What paying them off does to your credit score</h2>
    <p>
      Paying credit card balances down helps. Utilisation — how much of your available credit
      you are using — is one of the largest factors in a score, and it responds quickly, so a
      lower balance usually shows up within a billing cycle or two.
    </p>
    <p>
      The one thing that can work against you is <strong>closing the card afterwards</strong>.
      Closing it removes that card's limit from your total available credit, which pushes
      utilisation on everything else up, and over time it shortens the average age of your
      accounts. Keeping a cleared card open, unused, generally does more for a score than
      closing it does.
    </p>
    <p>
      Carrying a balance does <em>not</em> build credit. That is a persistent myth and an
      expensive one: the card reports activity whether or not you leave a balance on it, so
      paying in full each month gives you the same reporting without the interest. Paying a
      card off early is not penalised.
    </p>

    <h2>Why paying it down beats saving</h2>
    <p>
      If you carry a balance at 20% or more, clearing it is effectively a guaranteed, tax-free 20%
      return — more than almost any investment reliably earns. Prioritising high-interest debt is
      usually the highest-value thing you can do with a spare dollar. To see that same compounding
      working <em>for</em> you instead, use the
      <a href="/finance/compound-interest-calculator">compound interest calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It assumes a <strong>fixed monthly budget</strong> and a <strong>fixed rate</strong> on each card; real minimum payments shrink as the balance falls, and real rates can change.</li>
      <li>It assumes you <strong>stop adding new charges</strong> — fresh purchases restart the cycle.</li>
      <li>Interest is applied monthly. Most issuers compound daily, which costs slightly more than shown.</li>
      <li>It does not model <strong>fees</strong>, penalty APRs, or the effect of a missed payment.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For a general fixed-rate debt with a set term, see the
      <a href="/finance/payment-calculator">payment calculator</a>; to work out the rate a quoted
      payment implies, use the <a href="/finance/interest-rate-calculator">interest rate
      calculator</a>; and to grow savings once the cards are clear, try the
      <a href="/finance/savings-calculator">savings calculator</a>. This is general educational
      information, not financial advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
